tpma-object-fixed-firmware-reserved: TPMA_OBJECT bit 3 is reserved

Classification: profile setting.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Part 2, Table "Definition of (UINT32) TPMA_OBJECT
Bits"): "3 | Reserved | shall be zero"; Part 2: "When any reserved bit in an
attribute is SET, the TPM shall return TPM_RC_RESERVED_BITS."

This version of the reference defines bit 3 of TPMA_OBJECT as fixedFirmware
(a draft attribute that none of its code evaluates) and therefore does not
reject it when it unmarshals TPMA_OBJECT (reserved mask 0xfff0f001). In the
specification version tpm-rs implements bit 3 is reserved, so a public area
with it SET is TPM_RC_RESERVED_BITS when it is unmarshaled (as in ibmswtpm2
and, with the same profile patch, mstpm20 version 185), instead of failing
later with another response code (e.g. TPM_RC_SIZE). The bit is added to the
reserved mask. Found by differential testing.
diff --git a/TPMCmd/tpm/src/support/Marshal.c b/TPMCmd/tpm/src/support/Marshal.c
--- a/TPMCmd/tpm/src/support/Marshal.c
+++ b/TPMCmd/tpm/src/support/Marshal.c
@@ -333,7 +333,10 @@
     if(result == TPM_RC_SUCCESS)
     {
         // check that no reserved bits are set
-        if(*((UINT32*)target) & (UINT32)0xfff0f001)
+        // tpm-rs differential testing -- profile setting: fixedFirmware
+        // (bit 3) is reserved, as in the specification version tpm-rs
+        // implements.
+        if(*((UINT32*)target) & (UINT32)0xfff0f009)
             result = TPM_RC_RESERVED_BITS;
     }
     return result;
